#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:141
msgid "Writing Unit Tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:143
msgid ""
"Now let's write some tests using the :ref:`unit_testing` solution that can "
"help us test code directly on the target board. We will use the :ref:"
"`unit_testing_frameworks_unity` testing framework. Since there is no default "
"configuration for the :ref:`framework_stm32cube` framework, we will provide "
"a :ref:`unit_testing_frameworks_unity_custom_config`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:148
msgid ""
"Also, we need to create a new folder ``test`` where the tests and custom :"
"ref:`unit_testing_frameworks_unity` configuration (described next) will be "
"located:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:153
msgid ""
"We will use ``USART2`` on ``ST Nucleo-F401RE`` board because it's directly "
"connected to the STLink debug interface and in OS it can be visible as a "
"Virtual Com Port, so we don't need any additional USB-UART converter. To "
"implement the custom :ref:`unit_testing_frameworks_unity` configuration we "
"need to create two files ``unity_config.h`` and ``unity_config.c`` and put "
"them in the :ref:`projectconf_pio_test_dir` in the root folder of our "
"project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:160
msgid "Implementation of ``unity_config.h``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:196
msgid "Implementation of ``unity_config.c``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:276
msgid ""
"Now we need to add some test cases. Tests can be added to a single C file "
"that may include multiple tests. First of all, we need to add three default "
"functions: ``setUp``, ``tearDown`` and ``main``. ``setUp`` and ``tearDown`` "
"are used to initialize and finalize test conditions. Implementations of "
"these functions are not required for running tests but if you need to "
"initialize some variables before you run a test, you use the ``setUp`` "
"function and if you need to clean up variables you use ``tearDown`` "
"function. In our example, we will use these functions to accordingly "
"initialize and deinitialize LED.  ``main`` function acts as a simple program "
"where we describe our test plan."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:278
msgid ""
"Let's add a new file ``test_main.c`` to the folder ``test``. Next basic "
"tests for blinking routine will be implemented in this file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:280
msgid ""
"``test_led_builtin_pin_number`` ensures that ``LED_PIN`` has the correct "
"value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:281
msgid ""
"``test_led_state_high``  tests functions ``HAL_GPIO_WritePin`` and "
"``HAL_GPIO_ReadPin`` with ``GPIO_PIN_SET`` value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:282
msgid ""
"``test_led_state_low``  tests functions ``HAL_GPIO_WritePin`` and "
"``HAL_GPIO_ReadPin`` with ``GPIO_PIN_RESET`` value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/ststm32/stm32cube_debugging_unit_testing.rst:285
msgid ""
"2 sec delay is required  since the board doesn't support software resetting  "
"via ``Serial.DTR/RTS``"
msgstr ""

